A ternary in a view prop, on a target whose language has no ternary. NoTernary
hoists it into `var __lt0 string` plus a value-only `if`, placed before the
widget whose prop reads the temp -- two statements that sit among a container's
children and render nothing.

A joining container appends one part per child, so joining a part for either of
those two puts an empty string into the box: a blank line above every widget
whose prop was a ternary. rendersPart is the distinction, and the `if` half of
it is what this fixture pins -- the hoisted decl was already covered by
effect_settle_sites, the `if` by nothing, so returning true for a FromTernary
`if` failed no test in the tree.

Read the two statements below and what they are is plain: the temp is assigned
in the container's own scope and the widget below reads it. A grep for `__lt0`
cannot see whether a `contentChildren = append(...)` came between them.

deny out/go/bubbletea/model.go `\}\n\tcontentChildren = append\(contentChildren, content_1\)` -- the hoisted if renders nothing, so no part is joined for it

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import app "sngl:app"

output { go { bubbletea } }

component main ui {
    var on = false
    var label = ""

    vbox {
        text(value=on ? "yes" : "no")
        button(text="toggle", @click { on = !on })
        text(value=label)
    }
}

app.window {
    main
}
-- out/go/bubbletea/model.go --
